AIS (Automatic Identification System) Maritime Tracker
-------------------------------------------------------
Name: William Eykyn
Date: 1/31/26 (updated 6/10/26)
Project: AIS Maritime Tracking
Description: Python application for live AIS vessel tracking and interactive
             map visualization. Streams real-time vessel positions via WebSocket,
             plots them on an interactive map, and displays vessel details
             including dimensions, voyage info, and track history.

------ Features ------

- Live vessel positions streamed via AISStream WebSocket API
- Color-coded markers by AIS class:
    Red   = Class A (commercial vessels, cargo, tankers, passenger ships)
    Blue  = Class B (recreational craft, smaller vessels)
    Pink  = Long Range broadcast
- Click any vessel to see:
    - Speed, heading, course over ground
    - Vessel type, IMO number, call sign
    - Dimensions (LOA, beam, draft)
    - Destination and ETA
- Click "Learn more" in the popup for a full side panel with all details
- Click vessel again to reveal its track history (trail line + origin dot)
- Click anywhere on the map to hide trails and close the side panel
- Auto-reconnects if the stream connection drops
- Map regenerates every 5 seconds (not on every ping)

------ Setup ------

1. Obtain a free AISStream API Key
    - Go to: https://aisstream.io/documentation
    - Scroll to the section titled "Authentication"
    - Click "Get Started" at the top of the screen and sign in via GitHub
    - Generate a free API key

2. Create your environment file
    - Create a file called .env in your project folder containing:
        AISSTREAM_TOKEN=your_api_key_here
    - If you cloned the repo you can instead copy the example file:
        cp .env.example .env
      Then open .env and replace "YOUR_API_KEY_HERE" with your API key

3. Run the tracker

    Option A — Python (recommended, fully configurable)
    - Clone this repo and open config.py to set your bounding box:
        LAT_MIN, LAT_MAX, LON_MIN, LON_MAX
    - Create and activate a virtual environment:
        python3 -m venv venv
        source venv/bin/activate
    - Install dependencies:
        pip install -r requirements.txt
    - Run the tracker:
        python3 ais_maritime_tracker.py

    Option B — Docker (quick start, default bounding box: New England coast)
    - The Docker image uses a fixed bounding box. To monitor a different
      region, use Option A instead.
    - Pull and run the pre-built image:
        docker pull willeykyn/aismaritimetracker:latest
        touch map.html
        docker run --rm --env-file .env -v $(pwd)/map.html:/app/map.html willeykyn/aismaritimetracker

5. View the map
    - Open map.html in your browser
    - The map updates automatically every 5 seconds — just refresh to see
      new vessel positions and any newly detected vessels
